    "question": {
      ":question/id": "89b6b705-9f0e-5db1-8697-ddd230323ef6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $y$ given a fixed value of $x.$\n\n![](images/43/43a4bba76de6c26562df525aa2a9768915cd12d818b962aef33f0a05d4cdbe82.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](images/56/56c5742740ee29f51ad56ad0acca1bd48fa7e21a5762561a786214aa0f2c3857.png)",
